# The wine cellar's bricked-over rack (prop larders_bricked_rack / larders_open_niche): the Tarokka place
# castle_ravenloft_wine_cellar (treasure_spots dialogue castle_ravenloft/larders_cellar:niche). No roll is needed.

~ niche
if flag.larders_niche_opened
-> niche_open
endif
Narrator: At the very back of the cellar, one rack has been bricked over. The bricks are newer than the wall around them, and someone has pressed a row of corks into the mortar like buttons.
if flag.larders_niche_told
Narrator: The cook's work. He did say he was good with bricks.
endif
* (Pull the bricks down.) -> niche_break
* [Investigation DC 12] (Look the brickwork over first.) -> niche_study | niche_break
* Leave it walled up. -> END

~ niche_study
Narrator: The mortar was laid from outside, in one go, by somebody tidy. Low on one brick, scratched from the inside, is a single word: VINEGAR.
-> niche_break

~ niche_break
set larders_niche_opened
time +10
Narrator: The bricks come out one at a time. Behind them, in a rack's worth of space, a man in a good merchant's coat sits among his own bottles, long dead, with his ledger in his lap.
if treasure_at:castle_ravenloft_wine_cellar
Narrator: Under the ledger, wrapped in a fold of his coat as if he were keeping it warm, is something that was never a wine merchant's.
tarokka give castle_ravenloft_wine_cellar
endif
Narrator: His purse is still on his belt. The ledger's last page is a bill to the castle for six casks, marked paid in a different hand.
gold +35
give book 1
interject tag:pious: Rest now, whoever you were. You can stop holding your breath.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Delivered six casks, got marked paid, got bricked in. I'm never delivering to a castle again. Poor fellow.
-> END

~ niche_open
Narrator: The wine merchant sits among his bottles with his coat open and his ledger closed. There's nothing more he can sell anyone.
-> END
